= Expansion of Public Works

With the abolishment of War Loans at Vera, the Askew cabinet finally feels comfortable to expand funding for its recently enacted public works program. A billion Gold in bonds released by the government is used to fund the program, and money begins to flow in the economy again.

This marks a real turning point, and along with the abolishment of War Loans, the radicalizing effects of sky-high unemployment and the Etaluxia Treaty are diminished. The set of conditions that have enabled the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalists</span>'s meteoric rise begins to unravel, depriving them of the fuel that once drove their ascent.

- @public_works

@public_works
title: Great news!
on-arrival: labor_dissent -= 5; dufour_angry_bruning -= 25; lautenbach_adopted = 1; lautenbach_plan_time = time + 12; public_works_name = "Dietrich"; gold_left += 3; ddp_left += 2; lvp_left += 2; ddp_cohesion += 2; inflation += 2; budget -= 1; economic_growth += 2; commons_royalist -= 3; commons_ddp += 1; commons_unionist += 1; commons_traditionalist += 1; commons_lvp += 1; disfavored_royalist -= 5; disfavored_unionist += 2; disfavored_ddp += 2; disfavored_traditionalist += 2; disfavored_lvp += 2

Despite Askew’s longstanding belief that public works should follow the bottoming out of the depression, it appears he now sees that moment as having arrived—with the global recovery and the abolition of Commonwealth War Loans. Still, inflationary fears among the public have constrained any major expansion of the money supply, and the guild-masters within the coalition have fought tooth and nail to gut the bill’s more expensive provisions. Fortunately, their votes are not required to pass it.

> Chancellor [+ chancellor +]'s response is— public works?

